diff --git a/apps/zcode-cli/docs/permission-dispatch.md b/apps/zcode-cli/docs/permission-dispatch.md
index 0843cdf..b7e6f35 100644
--- a/apps/zcode-cli/docs/permission-dispatch.md
+++ b/apps/zcode-cli/docs/permission-dispatch.md
@@ -24,3 +24,32 @@ Acceptance uses AgentRuntime.executeTurn with controlled model replies, the real
 writer and a real local effect: Allow→Ask, Allow→Deny, approved Ask→Ask, and a subsequent fresh
 request through the original broker. Rules change during the awaited started-event publication,
 before the actual effect, rather than replacing the permission checker with a mock.
+
+## Native ACP Stop and process settlement
+
+The existing Host abort signal fences every ACP permission callback immediately, including
+callbacks arriving after `session/cancel` and approvals resolving after Stop. Their result is
+`cancelled`, never an automatic allowance or an old approval. The vendor still owns its tools;
+Fleet does not invent Host receipts for them. ACP protocol v1 requires pending permission
+requests to receive the cancelled outcome (local reference `302e6f7`, current official tip
+`43e664a0d901`, `docs/protocol/v1/prompt-turn.mdx` cancellation section).
+
+```text
+Host abort → permission fence → session/cancel → cancelled prompt / bounded failure
+Host close → transport fence → TERM → bounded KILL → confirmed process close → release / checkpoint
+```
+
+`AcpClient` owns one idempotent close promise. Native port cleanup waits for that promise and
+any setup already in flight; the Host's existing `await native.close()` remains the only
+release/continuation settlement path. Late callbacks cannot write into closed stdin. A missing
+exit confirmation is an explicit cleanup failure after a two-second TERM grace and a two-second
+KILL confirmation window. POSIX teardown targets the owned process group and confirms its
+disappearance even when the leader closes without inherited pipes. Windows targets the owned
+child only; descendants that escape the POSIX group are outside this teardown claim.
+
+Acceptance uses isolated real child processes: Stop followed by a late automatic permission,
+Stop while an approval is pending, repeated close, a TERM-resistant child, and cleanup during
+setup, option replies arriving after Stop, and descendants without inherited pipes. Existing
+ACP catalog, continuation, modes and errors must continue to pass. Shared catalog and
+vendor-login consumers await the same process barrier before reporting completion. This changes
+no credential, permission-mode, Session storage, UI or provider protocol authority.
